    "why_not_200": "the 100-take data says the 11th take onward buys less than 1 accuracy point each and the 21st onward less than 0.4; at N=100 take-noise is already 3.5% of the sitting floor, so takes 20-200 are polishing a quantity that a different day undoes entirely",
    "why_not_3": "N=3 keeps only 57.7% of the achievable gain (53.11% vs 79.83% at N=90, design D) and reaches only 95.6% of the cross-sitting ceiling",
    "how_to_spend_them": "scatter the takes -- 30 takes said in a row are needed to match 10 takes scattered across the sitting; and split the board across several days rather than piling repeats into one",
    "headline_caveat": "unit identity and recording time are confounded in this data (each unit owns its own stretch of 2026-08-23), so the 6-way accuracies are upper bounds; the prototype-convergence and variance numbers are the confound-free ones and they agree"
